WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:05.000 align:start position:0%
Saturn's rings are disappearing. At
first, scientists thought that Saturn's

00:00:03.100 --> 00:00:08.900 align:start position:0%
rings were as old as the planet, so over
4.5 billion [music] years long. But then

00:00:07.400 --> 00:00:12.800 align:start position:0%
they sent this spacecraft to study it,
and they found they're likely much

00:00:10.200 --> 00:00:16.000 align:start position:0%
younger, only about 100 million to 400
million years old, which sounds like a

00:00:14.200 --> 00:00:19.300 align:start position:0%
lot, but that's younger than sharks.
Sharks have been on Earth longer than

00:00:17.500 --> 00:00:23.300 align:start position:0%
Saturn has had rings. But the problem
now is Saturn's rings are made up of all

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:28.000 align:start position:0%
these icy particles that get ground up
by tiny mini meteors. And then the sun's

00:00:25.900 --> 00:00:32.000 align:start position:0%
rays give those tiny ice particles an
electric charge, which means uh-oh,

00:00:30.700 --> 00:00:35.100 align:start position:0%
they're suddenly feeling the pull of
Saturn's magnetic field, bending their

00:00:33.800 --> 00:00:39.000 align:start position:0%
neat little orbits, causing them to
slide toward the planet until gravity

00:00:37.400 --> 00:00:43.000 align:start position:0%
takes over, pulling the water into the
atmosphere, causing it to rain down. And

00:00:41.200 --> 00:00:47.700 align:start position:0%
in [music] 2017, the spacecraft measured
up to 10,000 kg of this stuff raining

00:00:45.700 --> 00:00:52.400 align:start position:0%
down on Saturn every second, meaning
those iconic rings could be gone in 100

00:00:50.200 --> 00:00:55.900 align:start position:0%
million years. But what if you dove into
Saturn? To see that, subscribe.

